{
  "requirement": "A definition glob MUST match a complete full name. `*` MUST match zero or more characters, including dots; all other valid name characters MUST match literally. An empty glob MUST be invalid. Every definition selection MUST use this matching rule, including requirements evidence inputs, CLI definition filters, and the authoring `references` tool, and MUST match against both requirements and named examples.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check `requirements.references.definition_globs`.\n\nAll selection entry points call the same `definition_glob::validate` and `definition_glob::matches` functions. The callers are `source_input` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs`, `references` and `referencing` in `src/authoring/tools.rs`, `status_markdown_with_coverage` and `info_markdown` in `src/commands.rs`, and `InputSelector::resolve` in `src/inputs.rs`. The matching rule is therefore pinned by the unit test, and each entry point has a test showing it applies that rule.\n\nObligations and the tests that cover them:\n\n- **Complete full-name match.**\n  - `src/definition_glob.rs#mod[tests]/fn[wildcard_crosses_dot_segments_and_matches_the_whole_name]` rejects both a prefix and a suffix around `foo.*.bar`. It also checks that `foo.bar.*` does not match `foo.bar`.\n  - `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirements_inputs_track_matched_declarations_in_full_name_order]` repeats the full-name check for evidence inputs.\n- **`*` matches zero or more characters, including dots.**\n  - The unit test matches `foo.*.bar` against both `foo.x.bar` and `foo.x.y.bar`.\n  - The evidence test matches `foo.*.bar` against `foo.a.bar` and `foo.a.b.bar`.\n  - The zero-character case is covered by `tests/commands.rs#fn[commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors]`. There, `status requirements.*ready` selects `requirements.ready` and also `requirements.xready`.\n- **Other characters match literally.** The unit test checks that the `.` in `foo.bar` does not match `x` (`fooxbar`). It also checks that `_` and `-` are literal, and that `foo[bar]` is rejected as invalid.\n- **An empty glob is invalid.**\n  - The unit test asserts `validate(\"\")` fails.\n  - The evidence test expects the error \"definition glob must not be empty\" for an empty evidence selection.\n  - `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[references_list_names_and_flattened_sites_without_recording_inputs]` asserts that an empty `selection` is rejected by the `references` tool.\n  - The CLI is tested with the invalid glob `[` and expects \"invalid definition glob\". It has no empty-string case, but the shared `validate` covers that input.\n- **Every selection uses the rule, and matches both requirements and named examples.**\n  - Evidence inputs: the evidence test above, and `tests/definitions.rs#fn[selections_match_requirements_and_examples_but_never_named_definitions]`, where `rules.*` selects `rules.case` (an example) and `rules.ready` (a requirement).\n  - CLI: `commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors`, plus the CLI part of the `tests/definitions.rs` test, covering both `status` and `info`. `tests/commands.rs#fn[verbose_status_lists_fixme_judgments_and_globs_scope_issue_groups]` also covers `status` selection.\n  - Authoring `habeas` requirements form: `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirements_reads_select_requirements_and_examples_but_not_definitions]` uses `requirements.*` and gets the example and the requirement.\n  - Authoring `references` tool: the `references_list_names...` test above.\n  - `ripgrep` and `ast_grep` `referencing` selections: `fn[ripgrep_referencing_searches_only_referencing_files_or_nodes]` and `fn[ast_grep_referencing_restricts_the_search_as_ripgrep_does]`.\n\nI found no unacknowledged gap. There are no `{fixme}` acknowledgments, and none were needed.",
